column32:
Actually, when I said 'update', I meant the functions that in standard languages that would modify the existing structure return a new one, but it's great you found update-in. That's a beautiful function. There's also assoc-in, which is basically just a more specialized assoc-in.<p>I've built cyclical graphs in Clojure. The simplest form looks like<p><pre><code>   {:nodes #{:a :b :c}
    :edges {:a #{:b}
            :b #{:c}
            :c #{:a}}}
</code></pre>
(for non-clojurians, {} is a map, and #{} is a set)
